\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 Using the quiz bowl client\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This program serves the purpose of collecting and sending wanted data collected from quiz bowl games. It also presents the data for a game in a scoreboard to be used by the players and spectators to easily track the score. This document outlines the process to use this program.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 Section 1:\

\fs26 Setup of server.py and bot.py\

\f1\b0 \
Before launching the server, the file needs to be configured. The file defaults to localhost:9999, but that would need to be changed to something else to work over the internet.\
I recommend using playit to help host the server, it avoids port forwarding and is free.\
Download the playit launcher and follow its instructions to add the device to the user\'92s account.\
Create a UDP tunnel on the device running the server and target an IP. The user can use any IPv4 address. It can be changed by editing client.py on lines 13 & 14.\
\
Launching server.py will create players.db. This is the database that the server is linked to.\
\
Before launching bot.py, the user needs to create a Discord bot to work with the file. This can be done through the Discord Developer Portal. This process is well documented other places, but all the user needs from that is the bot\'92s API key.\
There are two options to provide the API key to bot.py:\
	1. Create a file called .env inside of src, and writing KEY=[bot\'92s API key]\
	2. Replace line 27 with BOT_TOKEN = \'93[bot\'92s API key]\'94\
After adding the bot to a server, bot.py can be launched.\
There are three commands - /stats, /score, and /games. Discord members can prompt the bot to get their data.\
bot.py requires pdf.py to be placed in the same directory to function properly\

\f0\b\fs32 \
Section 2:\

\fs26 Preparing config.ini\

\f1\b0 \
config.ini holds variables that the client and gui need to function properly.\
It has the following format:\
[CONNECTION]\
ip = [user\'92s ip goes here]\
port = [user\'92s port goes here]\
[FORMAT]\
use_rich_text = [true or false]\
\
ip and port are the IP and port of the server. use_rich_text is a boolean that tells the client whether to use ANSI color escape codes
\f0\b\fs32 \
\
Section 3:\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\fs26 \cf0 Using client.py to record a game.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 After selecting 1 on the initial menu, the game data entry wizard is given to the user. \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The program requests some information before beginning the game.\
	- Number of tossups (each tossup has three bonus questions)\
	- Number of lightnings (or speed check questions)\
	- Number of players per team\
	- For each team\
		- Asks whether the team will have individual stats recorded\
			- Yes: The program asks for usernames to record data under. This allows for data to be broken down by player within a team.\
			- No: The program treats each player on the team as the same in the data.\
		- Directly asks for a team name (if one or both teams do not record individual stats) or asks if the user wants to enter team names (if both teams record individual stats)\
		- For teams that record individual stats, it asks for usernames for each seat.\
			- Usernames are unique and correspond to names within the database.\
			- When the username is entered, the user confirms the name if it is recognized in the database. If the username is not in the database, the program prompts the user to add the player.\
			- Continues until all seats are filled\
Once the game begins it follows the format of all tossups, substitution, then all lightnings\
	- Each tossup has 9 selections to choose from. 8 categories and the option to substitute.\
		- Substitution \
			- Asks for the username or seat ID of the person to be subbed out.\
			- Seat ID is two characters: the team letter and seat number (ex. 1a, b3, etc.)\
			- Asks for the username for the player to be subbed in.\
			- Replaces the subbed out player with the subbed in player.\
		- Category\
			- Question will be saved under that category.\
			- Asks for the username or seat ID for the player that buzzed, or pass if no player buzzed. Will refuse usernames not in the game or on a team that has already buzzed.\
			- After username is selected, it asks for if the player got the question for power, ten, neg, or zero.\
			- Game moves to bonus after, with the user choosing \'93c\'94 or \'93i\'94 for correct or incorrect.\
	- After tossups, the game moves into a substitution section, same as the option in the tossups.\
	- After the substitution option, the game moves into lightning rounds\
		- The program asks for the username or seat ID like for tossups. It then asks \'93c\'94 or \'93i\'94 like bonuses. Only one player can buzz on lightnings.\
		- No substitutions can be made during the lightning round.\
	- Tossups can be corrected during the next tossup. The last played tossup can be reverted to, but not any previous tossups. If the user subs on a tossup and then immediately reverts to the preceding tossup, those subs are erased.\
\

\f0\b\fs32 Section 4:\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\fs26 \cf0 Using gui.py in conjunction with client.py
\fs32 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 \
gui.py is a scoreboard for client.py\
The user can launch gui.py and enter the six-digit game code provided in client.py to connect to the game.\
The scoreboard displays the score, seats, question number, and the current history of the game.\
gui.py also requires config.ini\
\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
